e use of it they think that  mathematics is the basis of everything  and so you know this is an idea that  that has not died with the Pythagorean  school by any means we still see people  saying things like this at the present  so he says that through studying  mathematics they came to believe that  the principles the RTI the the you know  the beginning ruling factors you could  say were the principles of everything  not just mathematics but everything else  and we're going to talk about that in  just a moment what those look like says  since numbers are by nature first among  these principles number is Audrey's  moyer right he said that they fancy that  they could detect in numbers to a  greater extent than in the elements like  fire earth and water many analogs of  what is and comes into being so they  ended up extending the consideration of  number not just to the physical world  but also to moral matters or phenomena  he mentions for example a certain  property of number being justice another  being soul or mind and other being  opportunity Cairo Co the right time  right and so they they view all of these  things as either being numbers  themselves or as being functions or  modes of of numbers and they  systematically extend their theory to  try to cover everything this is another  central aspect of philosophy it attempts  to be systematic so he says since they  they saw that properties and ratios of  musical scales are based on numbers and  since it seemed clear that all of the  things have their whole nature modelled  on numbers and that numbers are the  ultimate things in the whole physical  universe they assumed the elements of  numbers to be the elements of everything  and the entire universe to be a  proportion or number so this is a  point of view and a project that has  very universal scope they really are  trying to extend their theory to  everything and I'll give you some  examples of what he tells us about them  giving a bit of summary he says the  elements of number are the even and the  odd of these the former is limited that  is the even and the odd are unlimited  unity consists of both since it's both  odd and even numbers derived from unity  and numbers comprise the whole universe  and then he said others of this same  school hold that there's ten principles  a decade right which they enunciate in a  series of corresponding pairs now notice  as we go through these that some of  these are going to be physical matters  and some of these are going to be moral  matters and there's others that are a  little bit harder to classify limits and  the unlimited okay very basic stuff odd  and even so here we're still in the  level of numbers unity and plurality  there's a pair as well right and left  male and female biological sex rest and  motion metaphysical principle straight  and crooked light and darkness let me  get to good and evil moral phenomena  square and oblong and so you know you  you might come up with all sorts of  other instances of numbers like that you  maybe there could have been 20 there  could have been any amount but what we  see here is an attempt to try to explain  everything on the basis of opposites and  numbers now Aristotle has some  criticisms one of the big criticisms  that he has is that they discussed and  defined the what of things the Peter  they discussed pierre e to TS dean that  the Totti esteem is what a thing is what  it is for it to be but he says they did  so superficially so that they didn't  really end up  explaining what things were he said they  suppose the essence of a thing is that  to which the term under consideration  first applies and so as an example of  this he says they thought that double  and the number two are the same and  they're clearly related to each other  right  but double and two are not exactly the  same thing  he says they thought this because two is  the first number which is double another  but you know eight is also a double a  double of four so doub